## Verify Configuration Before Boot

Before booting compute nodes, verify that boot-service and metadata-service are
configured correctly. These checks catch the most common failures before a
wasted boot cycle.

First, confirm that boot-service has the correct kernel parameters stored:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify boot-service boot config is uploaded -->
```bash
ochami -k boot config list -F yaml \
	| grep -q "ds=nocloud-net" \
	&& echo "boot-service: OK" \
	|| { echo "boot-service: FAILED - check boot config"; exit 1; }
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Next, confirm that the cloud-init default configuration was uploaded
successfully:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify cloud-init defaults are uploaded -->
```bash
ochami -k metadata defaults list -F yaml \
	| grep -q "base_url" \
	&& echo "cloud-init defaults: OK" \
	|| { echo "cloud-init defaults: FAILED"; exit 1; }
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Next, confirm that the compute group cloud-init configuration is present.
This is what delivers the SSH keys and munge key to the node at boot:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify cloud-init compute group is uploaded -->
```bash
ochami -k metadata group list -F yaml \
	| grep -qE "^ +name: compute$" \
	&& echo "cloud-init group: OK" \
	|| { echo "cloud-init group: FAILED"; exit 1; }
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Finally, confirm that the boot-service and the metadata-service both know each
compute node. These are the two lookups the node itself performs at boot, and
the only checks here that have to run after node discovery.

Neither service asks SMD when a node boots. Each answers from its own copy of
SMD, which it refreshes on a timer, so a node registered a moment ago is not
known to them yet:

- The boot-service syncs its nodes from SMD every 5 minutes by default
  (`hsm_sync_interval`, in minutes, in `/etc/openchami/configs/boot-service.yaml`).
  For a MAC it does not know yet, `/boot-service/bootscript` returns a script
  that prints "Node resolution failed" and halts, so a node that boots into that
  window stops and is not retried.
- The metadata-service syncs every 60 seconds by default
  (`--smd-sync-interval`). For an address it does not know, it still answers
  `200` on `/metadata-service/meta-data`, with the identity of another
  component. A node that gets this has no root SSH key and no munge key, and it
  never becomes reachable.

So check what each service returns, not the status: wait until the node's
bootscript loads a kernel and its meta-data names the node's own xname before
powering the nodes on. The wait can take up to the boot-service's sync interval.

The requests below are the ones the node will send. `X-Forwarded-For` carries the
node's address so that the metadata-service resolves the same entry; haproxy
appends its own entry to the header and the server reads the first.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify the boot-service and metadata-service know each compute node -->
```bash
for ((i=0; i<num_computes; i++)); do
	for ((t=0; ; t++)) ; do
		boot=0; meta=0
		curl -fsS -m 5 \
			"http://${sms_ip}:8081/boot-service/bootscript?mac=${c_mac[$i]}" \
			| grep -q "^kernel " && boot=1
		curl -fsS -m 5 \
			-H "X-Forwarded-For: ${c_ip[$i]}" \
			"http://${sms_ip}:8081/metadata-service/meta-data" \
			| grep -qx "instance-id: x1000c0s0b$((i+1))n0" && meta=1
		(( boot && meta )) && break
		(( t < 78 )) || {
			echo "pre-boot node: FAILED for ${c_name[$i]}" \
				"(boot-service=${boot} metadata-service=${meta})"
			exit 1
		}
		echo "Waiting for ${c_name[$i]}:" \
			"boot-service=${boot} metadata-service=${meta}"
		sleep 5
	done
done
echo "pre-boot node: OK"
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->
